  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCurrently, a deep trough is building in the Western U.S. with the \nridge axis located just to our east across the Mississippi Valley. \nAt the surface, a low pressure is developing over the Rockies with a \nwarm front extending across Nebraska early this morning. For the \nrest of today, strong southerly flow will continue to lead to above \nnormal temperatures across the region with highs topping out in the \nupper 60s and lower 70s. As the low deepens to our west, the \ntightening pressure gradient across western and central Kansas will \nlead to gusty conditions. Winds will continue to increase through \nthe morning with sustained winds between 25-30 mph and gusts to 40 \nmph for central Kansas locations. Elsewhere in the forecast area, \nsustained winds of 20-25 mph are expected with gusts to around 30 \nmph. Strong winds, above normal temperatures, and dry conditions \nwill lead to increased fire weather concerns this afternoon. Please \nsee the fire weather discussion for more information. Mild \nconditions will continue into the overnight hours with lows only \nfalling into the lower to middle 50s.\n\nFor Saturday, as the synoptic trough traverses the Northern Plains, \na mid/upper level short wave trough will lift across the Central \nPlains by late day. An expansive low pressure system and associated \ncold front will pass across the state. Ahead of this system, a plume \nof Gulf moisture will stretch across the Southern and Central \nPlains. The anomalous warmth and moisture combined with the potent \nfrontal boundary, will lead to widespread rain and thunderstorm \ndevelopment across the forecast area by Saturday afternoon,\ncontinuing into Saturday night. Despite the time of year, a few\nof these storms have the potential to reach severe criteria, \nprimarily across south-central Kansas where MUCAPE values will \nreach up to 500 J/kg with decent shear. Hail will be the most \nlikely hazard with the strongest storms. \n\nBehind the front for Sunday through the end of the forecast period, \nhigh pressure will build into the region. Much cooler conditions \nwill also settle in over the area with highs each afternoon reaching \ninto the 40s and overnight lows dipping into the 20s and 30s. A \nshort wave will sweep across the region on Tuesday. But given the \ndry air mass and dominant high pressure, minimal impacts are \nexpected. Cloudy skies and very light precipitation will be possible \nacross the western half of the state. Only trace amounts of precip \nare expected at this time. Otherwise, dry conditions and mostly \nclear skies are expected for the Sun-Thu time frame.",
